<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<speak version="1.1" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2001/10/synthesis" xml:lang="en-GB">
<!-- The Watch Below, Chapter 46: The Forbidden Verse. Point of view: Calder. -->
<p><s>Chapter Forty-six.</s></p><break time="800ms"/>
<p><s>The Forbidden Verse.</s></p><break time="1800ms"/>
<!-- p1 -->
<p>Old Fisk brought the paper up the river lane on the eighty-ninth day, inside his coat, because he could read and most of Harrow’s End couldn’t.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p2 -->
<p>He came into Bel’s yard at noon on his stick, red with cold, and stood by the byre door, and didn’t come in, and didn’t say good day. He took the paper out of his coat and held it out to Calder.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p3 · tension · Fisk -->
<p><prosody rate="97%">“Carter brought it over the ice from Millbrook last night,” he said. “Off the chapel door. There’s a dozen of them about. They read it out on the ice at Millbrook yesterday at the fair, in front of the whole town. There was a fight. The knights tried to take the man that read it.” He looked at the paper in his own hand as if it might bite him. “You’ll want to see it. It’s got you in it.”</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p4 -->
<p>Calder took it.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p5 -->
<p>He could read, a little. Enough to sign his name and count sacks and make out a broadsheet if he went slowly. He went slowly. He stood in Bel’s frozen yard with Mabbin’s long sad face over the byre door beside him and read it, word by word, with his lips moving, and Fisk stood and watched him do it and didn’t help.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p6 -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">Beneath the fortress-school of Greyhold.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p7 -->
<p>He read it twice. The second time he didn’t move his lips.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p8 -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">The last of the great beasts of the old realm. The Titan of the songs. That lay down below when the old ones went into the light.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p9 -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">Keep the watch below, and do not ask. It is a jailer’s oath.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p10 -->
<p>And at the bottom, under the two words he’d heard three thousand people shout on the Tidesteps at Wendmouth, a smaller part, that Fisk had said had him in it.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p11 -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">The old powers are waking. In the Marches, a farmer, one of the unchosen, has been chosen by three dragons in one night. Nobody has been chosen by three since the one realm. Ask yourselves why it has begun with him.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p12 · quiet -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">He stood in the yard with the paper in his hands.</prosody></p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p13 -->
<p>By dark, all of Harrow’s End had heard it.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p14 -->
<p>The ones who’d stayed. Wat Garrow, who came up the river lane to Upper Rush at dusk on the excuse of a borrowed saw and stood in Bel’s kitchen looking at Calder with his face very strange. The Ashby widow, who didn’t come but sent her girl. Two of the Hollins from the next farm, who came to the gate and stood there in the dusk and wouldn’t come in. Fisk, who’d gone back down to the Eelweir with another copy and read it out at the Wheel’s burned doorway to anybody who’d stand and listen, which was everybody.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p15 -->
<p>He didn’t need them to tell him what they were saying. He’d heard it on the Tidesteps. He’d heard it at the hedge after the battle.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p16 -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">It’s begun with him. Ask yourselves why.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p17 · Bel -->
<p>“Calder,” said Bel, at supper the next night. She hadn’t looked at him all day. “Is it true?”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p18 · Calder -->
<p>“Is what true?”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p19 · tension: halting, frightened · Bel -->
<p><prosody rate="97%">“Any of it. Is there a… thing. Under Greyhold. Under the river.” She was looking at her plate. Joss beside her was very still, his big hands on the table. “Is there something down there? You go out there. You’ve been on the ice. You’ve been on the island. Your dragons… Do they know? Is it true?”</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p20 -->
<p>He looked at his sister across the table.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p21 · dread -->
<p><prosody rate="96%">He thought of a night on the ice under the black shadow of the bank, a league above the island, and Aurveth lying flat with her throat pressed to the frozen river. Something vast. Bound. Turning. Wrong, like a bone set badly.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p22 · Aurveth (remembered) -->
<p><prosody volume="-3dB">Do not ask me. Not yet.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p23 · Calder -->
<p>“I don’t know,” he said.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p24 · reflective -->
<p><prosody rate="96%">It was true. He told himself it was true.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p25 -->
<p>Across the table, at the end of it, Ada had put down her spoon.</p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p26 -->
<p>She waited until the children were asleep.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p27 · tension: ritual, voice unsteady -->
<p><prosody rate="97%">Or until she thought they were. Pip and Nan in the loft with Wren, the three of them in a heap under the blankets like puppies. Ada went up the ladder and looked at them, and came down again, and went to the door and put the bar across it. She went to the shutters and closed them, both, and barred those too. She stood a moment at the hearth with her hand on the warm stone and said the hearth-words over it, low, the way she did every night. Calder had heard them ten thousand times. Tonight her voice wasn’t steady on them. Then she came back to the table, to her own chair, and sat down in it, and put her hands flat on the table in front of her.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p28 -->
<p>Bel and Joss looked at her. Calder looked at her.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p29 · quiet · Ada -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">“There’s a verse,” said Ada.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p30 -->
<p>Bel opened her mouth. Ada looked at her, and she closed it.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p31 · tender · Ada -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">“You know the song,” said Ada. “All of you. <prosody rate="90%">Before the six, there was one.</prosody> I’ve sung it to all of you. I sang it to you, Bel, in this kitchen, when you were teething and wouldn’t sleep. I sang it to Calder. I’ve sung it to Wren and to your two. Two verses and the hush. That’s how everybody sings it, on both banks. That’s how the mothers at Greyhold sing it to their babies, I shouldn’t wonder.”</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p32 · Calder -->
<p>“Ma,” said Calder.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p33 · quiet: a solemn confession · Ada -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">“Let me say it.” She didn’t look at him. “There’s a third verse. My grandmother taught it to me. My father’s mother, who came over the river from the Hearthmere side when she was a girl and never once went back. She taught it to me when I was Wren’s age, by her own fire, and made me say it back till I had it, and then she put my hand on her hearthstone and made me swear. Never to sing it. Not to anybody. Not until I saw what it was for.” Her hands were very still on the table. “She said if I sang it before then, I’d be calling something I couldn’t send back.”</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p34 -->
<p>The fire cracked in the grate.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p35 · Ada -->
<p>“I’ve kept that,” said Ada, “for fifty-one years.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p36 -->
<p>She looked at Calder then.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p37 · quiet · Ada -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">“I’ve seen what it’s for,” she said.</prosody></p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p38 -->
<p>She sang it sitting at the table with her hands flat on the wood and her eyes on the fire.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p39 · tender -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">She sang it the way she sang everything, low and a little rough, in her old voice that had sung every lullaby and every harvest song and every dirge in Harrow’s End for forty years. To the old tune. The same tune as the other two verses. He’d heard that tune every night of his childhood. He knew where it went. He knew where it rose and where it came down and where it hushed.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p40 · Ada (verse), singing, low and a little rough -->
<p>She sang the first verse. <prosody rate="90%" volume="-6dB">Before the six there was one, and the one was Winshire, and the old ones walked it unbound, by water and by fire.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p41 · Ada (verse), singing, low and a little rough -->
<p>She sang the second. <prosody rate="90%" volume="-6dB">The old ones went up into the light, and the one fell into six, and the last great beast lay down below to sleep in stone and dark.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p42 · awe -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">And where the hush should have come, she didn’t sing the hush. She went on. The tune went on, the same tune, into a place he’d never heard it go, and Ada’s voice went with it, very quiet.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p43 · awe: sung low, slow · Ada (verse), singing, very quiet -->
<p><prosody rate="95%"><prosody rate="90%" volume="-6dB">The last one sleeps where the oath is kept,</prosody> <prosody rate="90%" volume="-6dB">in iron and dark beneath the stone,</prosody> <prosody rate="90%" volume="-6dB">and the watch above has never slept,</prosody> <prosody rate="90%" volume="-6dB">and the watch has forgotten what it keeps.</prosody></prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p44 -->
<p>Nobody moved.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p45 -->
<p>The hush came next, but not the hush he knew. The same tune, the same rocking, a mother’s tune for a child who won’t sleep. Different words.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p46 · awe: lullaby rocking, ominous words · Ada (verse), singing, a rocking hush -->
<p><prosody rate="95%"><prosody rate="90%" volume="-6dB">Hush now, and do not ask it.</prosody> <prosody rate="90%" volume="-6dB">Hush now, and do not turn the key.</prosody> <prosody rate="90%" volume="-6dB">When two hands open what one hand shut,</prosody> <prosody rate="90%" volume="-6dB">it wakes, and it will not sleep again.</prosody></prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p47 -->
<p>She stopped.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p48 · quiet -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">The kitchen was absolutely silent.</prosody></p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p49 · Calder (thought) -->
<p><prosody rate="97%" volume="-4dB">Do not ask.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p50 -->
<p>He sat at the table with his hands round his cup and heard it go into him. For the third time. Twice on the ice at midwinter, and now here.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p51 · Ada (verse) -->
<p><prosody rate="90%">The last one sleeps where the oath is kept.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p52 -->
<p>He hadn’t known what the knights’ oath was until midwinter night. He’d lived his whole life across the river from Greyhold, and he’d known the knights swore something, at noon, facing the river; everyone on the Saltreach bank knew that, as you know your neighbour says his prayers. He’d never known the words. He’d never cared. On midwinter night he’d sat in the snow on the ice beside a dragon who wouldn’t speak, and the words had come across the river to him in the dark, from hundreds of voices on Greyhold’s wall.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p53 -->
<p><prosody rate="90%">Keep the watch below.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p54 -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">Beneath the fortress-school of Greyhold,</prosody> said the paper on the table, in his own slow reading, <prosody rate="95%">the crown of Hearthmere keeps in chains the last of the great beasts.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p55 · Ada (verse) -->
<p><prosody rate="90%">In iron and dark beneath the stone,</prosody> sang his mother. <prosody rate="90%">And the watch above has never slept. And the watch has forgotten what it keeps.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p56 -->
<p>He sat very still.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p57 · dread · Calder (thought) -->
<p><prosody rate="96%"><prosody rate="97%" volume="-4dB">It’s under them. Under Greyhold. That’s where it is. That’s what she touched, on the ice. That’s what’s lying down there, awake, turning, in iron and dark, with a whole school of knights standing on top of it every day at noon singing to it not to ask.</prosody></prosody></p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p58 -->
<p>Two leagues away, at Crow Scar, in the back of the cave, he felt Aurveth lift her head.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p59 -->
<p>She’d heard. All three of them had heard; they always heard what he heard, if it went into him hard enough, and this had gone into him like a nail. But it was Aurveth he felt. Her great head coming up off the stone in the dark, for the first time since the ice. The millwheel turning in her, turning, and then catching on something, and stopping.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p60 · quiet · Aurveth (bond, to Calder) -->
<p><prosody rate="95%"><prosody rate="94%" volume="-2dB">That is what I touched,</prosody> she said.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p61 -->
<p>That was all. Five words. Then nothing. He felt her put her head back down.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p62 -->
<p>But it was the first thing she’d said about it since the ice. It went into him and stayed.</p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p63 · Bel, very small -->
<p>“Ma,” said Bel.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p64 · quiet -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">Her voice was very small. She was sitting at the table with her hands over her mouth, staring at her mother. Joss beside her had put his arm round her.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p65 -->
<p>Ada didn’t look up. She sat with her eyes on the fire. She looked older than he’d ever seen her. Her hands lay on the table like something she’d set down.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p66 · Joss -->
<p>”<prosody rate="90%">When two hands open what one hand shut,</prosody>” said Joss. “What does that mean?”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p67 · Ada -->
<p>“I don’t know,” said Ada.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p68 · Joss -->
<p>”<prosody rate="90%">It wakes, and it will not sleep again.</prosody>”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p69 · dread · Ada -->
<p><prosody rate="96%">“I don’t know, Joss. I never knew. She never told me. She just taught me the words and made me swear.” She lifted her hands off the table at last and looked at them, as if they were somebody else’s. “I’ve never sung it. Never. Not once. Not even to myself, under my breath, hanging out washing. I was afraid to.”</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p70 · Calder -->
<p>“Why did you sing it now?” said Calder.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p71 -->
<p>His mother looked at him across the table.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p72 · tender: fierce love, steady voice · Ada -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">“Because they’ve put your name on it,” said Ada. “Those people with the rings. They’ve put your name at the bottom of a paper about a beast under the earth, and nailed it to the doors, and said it began with you.” Her voice didn’t change. “And I don’t know what’s under the earth. And I don’t know what my grandmother knew, or how. But I know my son. And I’ll not have you walking out into whatever this is not knowing the one thing I know.” She put her hands back on the table. “That’s what it’s for. I’ve seen it now.”</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p73 -->
<p>On the ladder to the loft, in the shadow, something moved.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p74 -->
<p>Calder looked up. Wren was sitting on the top rung in her nightgown with her knees drawn up and her arms round them. She’d been there, he understood, the whole time. Since the bar went across the door. Her face in the firelight was very white and very still, and she was looking at her grandmother.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p75 · tender: a child's wonder · Wren -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">“Gran,” she said. “That was the most beautiful one.”</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p76 · grief -->
<p><prosody rate="94%">Ada looked up at her. For a moment her old face didn’t do anything at all. Then it crumpled, all at once, like paper in a fire, and she put her hands over it.</prosody></p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p77 · Lisska (bond, to Calder) -->
<p><prosody rate="94%" volume="-2dB">The lights,</prosody> said Lisska.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p78 -->
<p>She’d been at the window. She’d come over from Crow Scar after dark, low over the Fellwood, without being asked, and was lying in Bel’s yard against the wall under the shuttered window with her long head beside the shutters, listening. He felt her lift her head.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p79 · urgent · Lisska (bond, to Calder), sharp, frightened -->
<p><prosody rate="105%"><prosody rate="94%" volume="-2dB">The lights,</prosody> she said again. Sharp. Frightened. <prosody rate="94%" volume="-2dB">Across the river. At the big grey place. Greyhold. All the lights. They’ve gone out.</prosody></prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p80 -->
<p>He was up from the table before he knew it, and across the kitchen, and lifting the bar from the door, and out into the yard.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p81 -->
<p>It was very cold. Bitter. The stars were sharp and the moon was coming up late and lopsided over the hills behind the house, and the white river went away below the hollow, frozen from bank to bank, down past Harrow’s End and the island to the bend where Greyhold stood.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p82 -->
<p>Greyhold was dark.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p83 -->
<p>He’d seen it every night of his life. The long low line of its wall on the far bank, six leagues and more down the river, with its windows lit. On midwinter night every window had been lit. On any night of the year, from Harrow’s End, from Upper Rush, from anywhere on the Saltreach bank where you could see down the river, Greyhold was there: a line of small steady lights in the dark, like a necklace laid along the far bank.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p84 -->
<p>It wasn’t there.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p85 · dread -->
<p><prosody rate="96%">The whole line of it was gone. Every window. Every lamp on the wall. Every torch at the gate. As if somebody had put a hand over it. There was the dark bank, and the dark sky, and the white river, and nothing.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p86 -->
<p>Lisska had come round the corner of the house and was standing beside him in the snow, very close, so close her wing was against his shoulder. He felt her shaking.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p87 · dread · Lisska (bond, to Calder) -->
<p><prosody rate="96%"><prosody rate="94%" volume="-2dB">They all went out,</prosody> she said. <prosody rate="94%" volume="-2dB">All at once. Just now. Just when she stopped singing. I was looking. They all went out together. Like a candle when you blow it.</prosody></prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p88 -->
<p>He stood in the yard and looked at the dark place on the far bank where Greyhold should be.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p89 -->
<p>Behind him, in the doorway, he heard Joss come out. And Bel. Ada came last, with her shawl round her, to stand in the door with the firelight behind her and look down the river.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p90 · Calder (thought) -->
<p><prosody rate="97%" volume="-4dB">Hush now, and do not ask it,</prosody> he thought. He didn’t mean to. It went round in him. <prosody rate="97%" volume="-4dB">Hush now, and do not turn the key.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p91 · Ada (remembered) -->
<p><prosody volume="-3dB">She said if I sang it before I saw what it was for, I’d be calling something I couldn’t send back.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p92 -->
<p>The dark went on.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p93 · tension -->
<p><prosody rate="97%">He didn’t know how long. Not long. As long as it would take to sing the hush through, slowly. He stood in the frozen yard and didn’t breathe. Lisska didn’t breathe. At Crow Scar, two leagues off, he felt Kharrak on his feet, and Aurveth with her head up, all of them looking down the river at the place where the lights should be.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p94 -->
<p>Then they came back.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p95 · awe -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">All at once. Every one. The whole long line of Greyhold’s wall, every window and lamp and torch, lit again on the far bank like a necklace laid down in the dark. As if nothing had happened.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p96 -->
<p>Nobody in the yard spoke.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p97 · tension · Joss, not very steadily -->
<p><prosody rate="97%">“It’s the cold,” said Joss at last. Not very steadily. “That’s all. Something to do with the cold. Their lamps don’t burn oil, they say. Some sort of magic. It’ll be the cold.”</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p98 -->
<p>Nobody answered him.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p99 -->
<p>Ada stood in the doorway with her shawl round her and looked down the river at the lights. Calder turned round and looked at her. In the firelight from the kitchen behind her, her face was very calm.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p100 · quiet · Ada, quietly -->
<p><prosody rate="95%"><prosody volume="-5dB">“I didn’t call it,”</prosody> she said. Quietly. To him. <prosody volume="-5dB">“Did I?”</prosody></prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p101 · tender -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">He went and put his arm round her, and she let him, which she almost never did, and they stood in the doorway together and watched the lights of Greyhold burn on the far bank, steady and small, as if they had always been there and always would be.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p102 · quiet · Aurveth (bond, to Calder), very far off, very low -->
<p><prosody rate="95%"><prosody rate="94%" volume="-6dB">That is what I touched,</prosody> said Aurveth, very far off, very low.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p103 -->
<p>He didn’t ask her what she meant.</p>
<break time="1800ms"/>
</speak>
